perhaps the most famous institution  throughout the history of East Germany  was its secret police force the ministry  for state security better known as the  Stasi now the Stasi a well known mostly  because of how incredibly effective they  were at their jobs that being the  suppression of dissent spying on the  broader East German population and  conducting espionage across the Western  world but how did the Stasi work where  did it come from and what happened to it  after the reunification of Germany well  the Stasi was founded in nineteen fifty  one year after East Germany itself with  the man called Vilhelm Zeisler at the  helm size' tried to remove the East  German first secretary Volta Oh bricked  in 1953 but failed and so was himself  ousted he was replaced by ernst von  Weber who was shortly after was replaced  himself by Arish Milka who kept his job  until 1989 all three of these men were  former Soviet intelligence agents and  were seen to be low to Moscow which was  the point the Intelligence Agency  founded across the Eastern Bloc at this  point were first and foremost loyal to  the center of the Communist world and  not their own nations the Stasi was  founded not to protect East Germany  itself but to protect the interests of  the socialist Unity Party therein  although those in charge would have  undoubtedly argued that those were the  same thing in fact one of the issues the  Stasi had at the beginning of its  inception was that it could only recruit  fresh people and not intelligence  veterans why well because all of  Germany's previous intelligence officers  had worked for the Third Reich and thus  were seen as untrustworthy so how was  the Stasi organized well Milka stood at  the top of the Stasi and reported  directly to the Communist leadership  from the Stasi headquarters in East  Berlin  most of his operations were focused on  East Germany itself except for one  section the hva which conducted  international espionage this was mostly  focused on West Germany and ultimately  this is something the Stasi were very  good at it operated his own well trained  guards unit maintained jails for  political prisoners and spied on  soldiers to make sure none of them  disagreed with the government of course  the Stars his primary role was to spy on  the East German population and boy did  it at its height in the 1980s the Stasi  had over 90,000 official employees  backed up by several hundred thousand  informants out of a population of just  over 16 million those who complained too  often or was seen as being outright  hostile to the communist leadership  would often find themselves as political  prisoners the Stasi also routinely  intercepted and monitored communications  between people especially those between  East and West Germany and it focused a  lot of resources on catching people who  were illegally consuming West German  media the level of surveillance in East  Germany was remarkably high in most  citizens there were  point or another on the receiving end of  Stasi inquiries which is why it was so  hated by the East German population yet  despite staggering levels of  surveillance the Stasi were unable to  prevent to predict the 1989 revolution  and the reason why was that they  overestimated the commitment of everyday  East Germans to communism the Stars he  assumed that membership of the social  Unity Party meant that a person liked  communism whereas actually people joined  it to advance their careers  thus when Gorbachev left East Germany to  its own devices and the people there  started to demand reforms the Stasi  thought that it wouldn't go anywhere  thus when party members and leading  officials started to demand change  particularly with respect to the  security forces the Stasi were  blindsided after reunification those who  would work for the Stasi were for the  most part fired and there were attempts  to prosecute Stasi higher-ups but the  vast majority of these went nowhere in  1990 the Stasi was disbanded yet its  legacy and the reputation of its workers  is still fiercely debated in Germany  today I hope you enjoyed this episode  and thank you for watching with extra  thanks to these patrons you can see on  screen for their continued support and  an extra special thanks to James  Bissonnette David archaeologists as ARCA  Flash Danny Maloney party-boy Koch  coolin Castleman Rob Waterhouse Chris  wicker Gustav Swan David Silverman  Winston Kate woods Christian checky  Maggie packs Kowski Lexi Schwinn Anthony  Beckett  Robert Wetzel Skye Chappell Mike  spinning three plates are on the white  and Michael Reynolds
 

